The `ModelClient` handles the actual routing based on the model string.

The core routing logic is in `detectProvider` (lines 152-159):

```typescript
const detectProvider = (model: string): string => {
    if (model.startsWith('gemini:')) return 'gemini';
    if (model.startsWith('huggingface:')) return 'huggingface';
    if (model.startsWith('fireworks:')) return 'fireworks';
    if (model.startsWith('openrouter:')) return 'openrouter';
    if (model.startsWith('@cf/') || model.includes('cloudflare')) return 'cloudflare';
    return 'ollama';
};
```

This confirms that:
1.  If the model name starts with `huggingface:`, it goes to the `callHuggingFace` method (which uses the HuggingFace API).
2.  If it doesn't match any of the cloud prefixes, it defaults to `ollama` and goes to the `callOllama` method.

